Give the batched Coulomb tests one cell per batch.

Three tests in `TestBatchedCalculations` declare two batches and supply one
cell:

    cell      = torch.tensor([[[100., 0, 0], [0, 100., 0], [0, 0, 100.]]])
    batch_idx = torch.tensor([0, 0, 1, 1])

The kernel indexes the cell per atom -- `coulomb.py`, in each of the four
batched variants:

    system_id = batch_idx[atom_i]
    cell_t = wp.transpose(cell[system_id])

so atoms 2 and 3 read `cell[1]` of a length-1 array.  Warp does not
bounds-check in release mode, and the read runs off the end of the allocation.

**Whether that is visible is luck**, which is why this sat unnoticed.  The
cell reaches the arithmetic only through the periodic shift:

    shift_vec = cell_t * type(ri)(unit_shifts[edge_idx])
    r_ij = ri - rj - shift_vec

and every `unit_shift` in these three tests is zero.  `0 * garbage` is `0`
while the garbage decodes as an ordinary double, so the wrong cell costs
nothing and the test passes.  When those bytes decode as NaN or inf it is
`0 * NaN`, and from there `r = NaN`, the guard `if r >= cutoff or r < 1e-10`
is False for NaN, and the NaN lands in the force accumulator:

    assert torch.allclose(forces[0], forces[2], rtol=1e-10)
    E   where forces[0] = tensor([1., 0., 0.])
    E     and forces[2] = tensor([nan, nan, nan])

Batch 0 is right because `cell[0]` is real; batch 1 is NaN because `cell[1]`
is not.  Only `test_two_independent_batches` has been seen to fail, under
pytest-xdist with 32 workers; the other two make the same out-of-bounds read
and have so far been lucky.  Nothing about this is specific to an interpreter
version or a platform -- it depends on what happens to sit past the end of a
tensor.

`test_batch_momentum_conservation`, in the same class and also two batches,
already passes a cell per batch.  This makes the other three match it.

Worth a second look upstream: nothing validates `cell.shape[0]` against
`batch_idx.max()`, so a caller making this mistake in earnest gets silent
corruption rather than an error.  That is a library change rather than a test
one, and is not attempted here.

